    "description": "Esfahan Steel Company is an Iranian steelworks whose development formed part of the industrial transformation of the Isfahan region. Historical research explains its location, while an engineering contractor documents a later modernization of its blast-furnace equipment. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Steel", "Isfahan", "Industrial history", "Blast furnaces"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T13:18:16Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T13:18:16Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Esfahan Steel Company: Origins and Furnace Modernization | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore the historical setting of Esfahan Steel Company and a documented blast-furnace modernization, with clear limits on current evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "historical-setting",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A steelworks shaped by its location",
        "content": "Esfahan Steel Company belongs to the history of state-supported industrial development around Isfahan, a city whose modern economy expanded beyond its older manufacturing trades. Historian Habib Borjian records that an agreement with the Soviet Union to construct a steel mill near Isfahan was reached in 1966. The selected site lay southwest of the city, near Chamgordan and Varnamkhvast. [s1]\n\nThe location reflected a balance between industrial requirements. Access to the Zayandeh Rud river and the workforce of its densely populated valley favored this site, even though essential raw materials came from elsewhere. Borjian identifies coking-coal deposits north of Kerman and iron-ore deposits around Bafq as important supply locations. The resulting geography connected the steelworks to a wider mining and transport system. [s1]\n\nThis historical explanation should not be read as evidence of present water availability or environmental performance. It explains the considerations behind the original location; assessing the plant today requires contemporary evidence about water withdrawals, recycling, emissions, and production. The historical source does not establish those outcomes. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "documented-modernization",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a furnace upgrade establishes",
        "content": "METPROM, an engineering contractor, identifies Esfahan Steel Company as the customer for a blast-furnace modernization project associated with 2013. Its project record describes work on Blast Furnace No. 2, including a bell-less charging device and changes to the skip bridge used to deliver material to the furnace top. These are specific elements of furnace operation, rather than evidence of a complete replacement of the steelworks. [s2]\n\nThe record also lists work involving the furnace shell, cooling system, casthouse, and a pump station for chemically treated water. Together, these details show that modernization concerned both the furnace and supporting equipment. Because the account comes from a participating contractor, it is useful primary evidence of the reported engineering scope. It is not an independent assessment of subsequent reliability, environmental benefits, or commercial results. [s2]\n\nThe distinction between design specifications and measured production is especially important. A contractor's stated design capacity cannot substitute for an annual company production series. This page therefore does not convert the project specifications into a chart of company output. Establishing current operations requires dated company disclosures and independent corroboration beyond the inspected project record. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "title": "Isfahan xiv. Modern Economy and Industries (2) Isfahan City",
