    "description": "Iran's copper industry connects mineral deposits in regions including Kerman and Azerbaijan with industrial production. Its history encompasses early metalworking and the development of large mining workplaces, while national statistics distinguish ore, concentrate, smelting, and refining. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Copper", "Mining", "Kerman", "Sarcheshmeh", "Industrial labor"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T13:18:16Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T13:18:16Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran's Copper Industry: Geography, Labor and Production | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran's copper regions, the historical development of mining labor, and a carefully defined national production series for 2020–2024."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "geography-and-history",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From early metalworking to industrial mining",
        "content": "Copper has a long history in the territory of present-day Iran. Encyclopaedia Iranica describes archaeological evidence of extraction and metalworking at sites including Sialk near Kashan and Tall-e Eblis in Kerman. Such evidence establishes early metallurgical activity; it does not imply uninterrupted operation of a modern industry at those places. [s1]\n\nFor the modern sector, geography matters more than a single national label suggests. The same reference identifies important deposits in Kerman, including Sarcheshmeh and Meiduk, and in Azerbaijan, including Sungun. These names identify distinct deposits and production locations. Their output should not be treated as interchangeable with that of the country as a whole. [s1]\n\nThe geological account also describes copper occurring alongside other potentially valuable elements, including molybdenum, gold, and silver in porphyry deposits. A porphyry deposit is a mineralized geological system associated with intrusive igneous rocks. Its economic significance depends on more than the gross weight of rock: metal content and recoverability matter. Historical resource figures in this reference are not used here as current reserve estimates. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "labor-and-industrialization",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Industrial development was also a change in working life",
        "content": "The establishment of a major mine creates a workplace as well as a production facility. Historian Abdolreza Alamdar's study of Sarcheshmeh examines the transition from worker-peasant livelihoods to industrial employment during 1966–1979. Its publicly inspected abstract identifies management control, paternalism, bargaining, dispute resolution, welfare policy, and workers' agency as central subjects. [s3]\n\nThis perspective broadens an account otherwise dominated by deposits and tonnage. Production growth cannot, by itself, establish how benefits were distributed or how workers experienced industrialization. The abstract supports identifying the questions and historical scope of the study; it does not support detailed claims about findings available only in the full article. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-production",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading the production figures",
        "content": "The series below measures copper contained in mine concentrates. It excludes the separately reported solvent-extraction series and must not be presented as refined copper or gross ore output. USGS reports these production stages separately; adding them would double-count material moving through the industry. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
