    "description": "Iran's aluminum industry includes primary-metal producers and an upstream alumina industry. Understanding its statistics requires distinguishing mined bauxite, refined alumina, and aluminum metal, which are different stages of production. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Aluminum", "Alumina", "Smelting", "Electricity", "Nonferrous metals"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T13:18:16Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T13:18:16Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran's Aluminum Industry: Production and Processing | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Distinguish bauxite, alumina and primary aluminum, explore the production process, and examine Iran's reported national output for 2020–2024."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "iranian-industry",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An industry with several production stages",
        "content": "Iran's primary aluminum producers include SALCO, Iranian Aluminum Company, Almahdi Aluminium Company, and Iran Alumina Company, according to the USGS review of the sector. Their individual production figures in that review use an Iranian fiscal reporting period; they should not be mixed directly with the Gregorian-year national series presented below. [s1]\n\nThe national series rises through 2023 before falling in 2024. It measures primary metal, excluding recycled aluminum as a separate production category. The figures describe output, not installed capacity, profitability, or domestic consumption. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "process-explained",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why bauxite, alumina, and aluminum are different",
        "content": "The production chain begins with bauxite, an ore from which alumina is chemically extracted. Alumina is aluminum oxide, an intermediate material rather than finished metal. In primary production, the Hall–Héroult process uses electricity to separate aluminum from oxygen in a molten bath. The Aluminum Association distinguishes this route from secondary production, which recycles existing metal. [s2]\n\nInside the smelting cell, alumina dissolves in molten cryolite. Carbon anodes conduct current into the bath; oxygen from the alumina combines with carbon, while molten aluminum collects below. The metal is removed and cast. This explains why an aluminum smelter is technically different from an alumina refinery, even when the two belong to the same industrial group. [s2]\n\nThe distinction matters when comparing national statistics. Tonnes of mined ore, alumina, and metal measure different materials at different stages. They cannot be added to create a meaningful total of aluminum production. Nor does domestic smelting output alone establish that the country supplies all its own upstream material. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "primary-production-process",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "The primary aluminum production chain",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[\"Bauxite ore\"] -->|\"Chemical extraction\"| B[\"Alumina\"]\n  B -->|\"Feed to electrolysis\"| C[\"Hall-Heroult cell\"]\n  D[\"Electricity and carbon anodes\"] -->|\"Inputs\"| C\n  C -->|\"Metal collection\"| E[\"Molten aluminum\"]\n  E -->|\"Casting\"| F[\"Primary metal ingots\"]\n```",
        "summary": "Arrows show material processing and essential inputs. This is a general production explanation, not a map of verified supply relationships among Iranian facilities. [s2]",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "energy-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Electricity and environmental performance",
        "content": "SINTEF, a Norwegian research organization, describes aluminum electrolysis as energy-intensive and identifies cell design, process control, energy efficiency, and emissions as important research areas. Its account also explains that environmental concerns include gases, by-products, and residual materials. [s4]\n\nThese process characteristics explain why a national output chart gives only a partial picture of an aluminum industry. Evaluating Iranian facilities would require evidence about electricity supply, energy consumption, operating interruptions, emissions controls, and actual releases. General technical descriptions cannot establish the performance of a particular plant. This draft therefore makes no unsupported claim about the carbon intensity or environmental compliance of Iranian production. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "primary-metal-trend",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iran: primary aluminum metal production, 2020–2024",
          "dataset_id": "primary-aluminum",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["primary-metal"],
          "x_axis_label": "Gregorian reporting year",
          "y_axis_label": "Metric tonnes of primary aluminum",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "Reported primary-metal production increased from 2020 through 2023, then declined in 2024 while remaining above its 2020 level. [s3]"
        },
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
